window.NEXY_SEARCH_INDEX = {"en":[{"u":"en/getting-started/introduction/","t":"What is Nexy?","s":"Getting started","k":["introduction","what is nexy","about","philosophy","full stack","python framework","fastapi"],"b":"Nexy (pronounced /neksi/) is a full-stack Python web framework built on FastAPI and Starlette. It brings the developer experience of modern frameworks to Python. The name stands for New eXperience for PYthon - a fresh way to build web applications, from your first route to the final deployment. tip Why Nexy? Two routers to choose from: file-based routing and modular routing. Pages are plain files (`.nexy`): Python logic on top, template below. Smart defaults - a project runs with almost no configuration. Great developer experience: dev server with hot reload, browser error overlay, clean terminal output. Philosophy in one sentence Nexy doesn't reinvent the wheel. It makes Python web development radically simpler while keeping the full power of FastAPI. Ready? Let's create your first project on the quick start page."},{"u":"en/getting-started/installation/","t":"Installation","s":"Getting started","k":["install","pip","uv","requirements","python version","getting started"],"b":"Install Nexy with your favorite Python package manager. You need Python 3.12 or newer. Check Python python --version # Python 3.12.x or newer Install with pip pip install nexy uv pip install nexy tip Verify the installation nexy --version # nexy 2.x Now that Nexy is installed, move on to creating your first project."},{"u":"en/getting-started/quick-start/","t":"Create your first project","s":"Getting started","k":["quick start","create project","nexy new","first app","hello world","scaffold","template"],"b":"Scaffold a complete project in one command, then start the dev server. 1. Create the project Run nexy new and answer the questions (name, router type, database). Or pass a template directly: nexy new myapp --template web-fbr-react # or interactively: nexy new myapp note 2. Start the dev server cd myapp nexy dev Open http://localhost:3000. You should see your brand-new homepage. Edit a file in src/routes/ and watch the browser update - no reload needed. 3. What you got myapp/ ├── nexyconfig.py # your app configuration ├── src/ │ └── routes/ # file-based routes (FBR) │ ├── index.nexy # -> / │ └── layout.nexy # shared layout Learn more about this layout on the project structure page."},{"u":"en/getting-started/project-structure/","t":"Project structure","s":"Getting started","k":["structure","src","routes","apps","folders","files","layout","nexyconfig"],"b":"Nexy follows a simple convention: everything lives under src/. This page explains each folder for both router types. File-based routing project (web-fbr) myapp/ ├── nexyconfig.py # configuration (ports, aliases, frameworks...) ├── public/ # static assets served as-is └── src/ ├── routes/ # each file becomes a route │ ├── index.nexy # -> / │ ├── about.nexy # -> /about │ ├── layout.nexy # shared layout, applied to child pages │ └── users/ │ ├── index.nexy # -> /users │ └── [slug].nexy # -> /users/:slug ├── models/ # your data models └── services/ # business logic Modular routing project (web-modular) myapp/ └── src/ └── apps/ ├── app_module.py # the app module (imported in nexyconfig) ├── app_controller.py # routes as decorators ├── app_view.nexy # the template ├── app_service.py # business logic ├── app_model.py # data model ├── layout.nexy # shared layout └── users/ ├── user_module.py ├── user_controller.py ├── user_service.py ├── user_model.py └── user_view.nexy Key files File Role nexyconfig.py Application configuration. See [[configuration|#/en/config/nexyconfig]]. src/routes/ Routes defined by the file tree (FBR). src/apps/ Routes defined with modules and decorators (modular). "},{"u":"en/cli/overview/","t":"Command overview","s":"CLI","k":["cli","commands","terminal","nexy command","overview","list"],"b":"The nexy command-line tool (binary nx) manages the whole lifecycle of your project. nexy --help Command summary Command What it does nexy new <name> Creates a new project in a new directory. nexy init Sets up the current directory as a Nexy project. nexy dev Starts the dev server with hot reload. nexy build Builds the app for production (SSG/SSR). nexy start Starts the production server. nexy add <item> Adds a component or module to your app. nexy migrate Runs database migrations. nexy translate Translates your content files. tip"},{"u":"en/cli/dev-server/","t":"The dev server","s":"CLI","k":["dev","server","hot reload","hmr","watcher","localhost","port","overlay","errors"],"b":"nexy dev is your everyday companion. It watches your files and gives you instant feedback. Start it nexy dev # or with a custom port: nexy dev --port 8080 What happens Your configuration is loaded from `nexyconfig.py`. A file watcher monitors `src/routes/` and related files. The client bundles are served through Vite (or esbuild) with hot module replacement. A browser error overlay shows compilation failures directly in the page. Keep in mind The default port is `3000` - override it in `nexyconfig.py` with `usePort` or with `--port`. Cache directories such as `__pycache__` are cleaned automatically. If a port is taken, Nexy tries the next available one. note"},{"u":"en/cli/new-init/","t":"new & init","s":"CLI","k":["new","init","create","project","scaffold","template","already initialized","rollback"],"b":"Two commands create projects: new (fresh directory) and init (current directory). nexy new Creates a brand-new project in a new directory. Works interactively or with a template: nexy new blog nexy new blog --template web-modular-vue Template sources `web-fbr` / `web-modular` / `api-fbr` / `api-modular` are bundled with Nexy and always work offline. Per-framework templates (e.g. `web-fbr-react`) are fetched from the Nexy GitHub repository. tip nexy init Turns the current directory into a Nexy project. If the directory is already a Nexy project, the command stops with exit code 1 instead of overwriting anything. mkdir myapp && cd myapp nexy init Next: understand the routing of your new project."},{"u":"en/cli/build-start/","t":"build & start","s":"CLI","k":["build","start","production","static","ssg","ssr","output","dist"],"b":"nexy build prepares your app for production; nexy start serves it. Build nexy build Nexy renders your pages (static generation per page) and bundles the client. The result is written to the build output directory. Each page is built with esbuild - one page never blocks another. If a page fails, it becomes a warning with the real error message, not a total failure. Client-only pages still get a placeholder `<div>`. Start nexy start # custom port/host: nexy start --port 8080 --host 0.0.0.0 Serves the built application with the production server. note"},{"u":"en/routing/overview/","t":"Routing overview","s":"Routing","k":["routing","router","routes","overview","choose","file based","modular"],"b":"Nexy offers two routers. Both are powerful; they just express routes differently. File-based routing (FBR) Routes come from your file tree inside src/routes/. Each .nexy, .mdx, .py (or .tsx, .vue, .svelte) file maps to a URL. Perfect for docs, marketing sites and APIs built by convention. Modular routing Routes are declared inside src/apps/ with modules and decorators. Each app groups controller, service and model. Perfect for larger applications with clear domain boundaries. Which one should I pick? You are building… Recommendation A docs site, landing page, or small MVP File-based routing A content-heavy site, blog, API-only project File-based routing A large app with domains and many developers Modular routing A REST API with controllers and services Either - both shine here tip"},{"u":"en/routing/fbr/","t":"File-based routing","s":"Routing","k":["file based routing","fbr","src/routes","index","slug","dynamic","nested","layout","mdx","tsx"],"b":"With file-based routing, the file tree IS the URL tree. Create a file, get a route. A file per URL src/routes/ ├── index.nexy -> / ├── about.nexy -> /about ├── blog/ │ ├── index.nexy -> /blog │ └── [slug].nexy -> /blog/{slug} └── api/ └── users.py -> /api/users (GET, POST...) Dynamic segments Folders or files in square brackets become URL parameters. In [slug].nexy, the route /blog/hello exposes a slug parameter with value hello. Nested layouts A layout.nexy in a folder wraps every page below it. It's the perfect place for a shared navbar or footer. Supported extensions `.nexy` - pages with Python logic and templates. `.mdx` - markdown pages. `.py` - API endpoints (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE). `.tsx` / `.vue` / `.svelte` - frontend framework pages. note"},{"u":"en/routing/modular/","t":"Modular routing","s":"Routing","k":["modular","modules","decorator","controller","service","model","apps","app_module"],"b":"Modular routing organizes your app into self-contained modules under src/apps/. Anatomy of an app src/apps/users/ ├── user_module.py # ties everything together ├── user_controller.py # routes, written as decorators ├── user_service.py # business logic ├── user_model.py # data model └── user_view.nexy # template The module The module declares the controller (and its routes) to Nexy. It is referenced from nexyconfig.py via useRouter. from src.apps.app_module import AppModule class NexyConfig(NexyConfigModel): useRouter = AppModule The controller A controller is a Python class whose methods are routes. Decorators tell Nexy which HTTP method and path each method handles. Why modular? Every domain is a folder: model, service, controller, view together. Great for teams and large codebases. Clean separation of concerns (SOLID-friendly)."},{"u":"en/routing/router-choice/","t":"Router choice","s":"Routing","k":["router choice","compare","fbr vs modular","when to use","decide","migrate"],"b":"A head-to-head comparison to help you pick your router with confidence. File-based routing (FBR) URL comes from the file path - zero configuration. Pages live close to their content (markdown, layouts, APIs). Best for: docs, landing pages, blogs, MVPs, API-only projects. Team habit: convention over configuration. Modular routing URLs are declared in code with decorators. Every domain is grouped: controller + service + model + view. Best for: large applications, clear business domains, many developers. Team habit: explicit structure over implicit naming. Quick decision guide Situation Choose 'I want a page for every markdown file' FBR 'My app has domains like billing, users, admin' Modular 'I'm prototyping an idea' FBR 'I'm building a long-lived product' Modular 'I only serve an API' FBR tip"},{"u":"en/nexy-files/syntax/","t":".nexy syntax","s":".nexy files","k":[".nexy","syntax","file format","frontmatter","python","template","jinja"],"b":"A .nexy file is a page: Python logic on top, template below, separated by a --- delimiter. Anatomy of a .nexy file --- # Python logic runs here title = \"Hello world\" items = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"] --- <!-- Your template goes here --> <h1>{ { title } }</h1> <ul> {% for item in items %} <li>{ { item } }</li> {% endfor %} </ul> Frontmatter The block between the opening --- is real Python. Variables you define there are available in the template below. The template The part after the closing --- is rendered with Jinja2. Use {{ }} to print values and {% %} for logic. Frontmatter config keys You can also set page-level options at the top of the frontmatter, like active or title, which Nexy reads for layout and metadata. note"},{"u":"en/nexy-files/components/","t":"Components inside .nexy","s":".nexy files","k":["component","reusable","include","import","template","link","form","image","nexy component"],"b":"Nexy ships a set of built-in components: Link, Image, Form, Audio, Video and more. Built-in components Component Use it for Link Internal navigation between your pages. Image Optimized images. Form Forms with session and CSRF helpers. Audio / Video Media embedding. Template Inline templating. Import Importing client-side modules. Vite Using a Vite-managed client asset. Example --- from nexy import Link, Image --- <Link href=\"/about\">About us</Link> <Image src=\"/logo.png\" alt=\"Logo\" /> Custom components Components are just reusable pieces of markup and logic. Put repeated UI in src/components/ and import it with the @ alias if configured. tip"},{"u":"en/hooks/overview/","t":"Hooks overview","s":"Hooks","k":["hooks","use","reactivity","server","state","client","overview"],"b":"Hooks are Python functions prefixed with use that give you access to the current request, navigation, sessions and more. Available hooks Hook Gives you useQuery The query parameters of the current URL. useRouter The router, for navigation. usePathname The current path. useSearchParams Search params as a dict. useSession The current session object. useCookies The request cookies. useToc The table of contents of the current page. useViews Page view tracking. useLocale The active locale. Example --- from nexy import useQuery, usePathname query = useQuery() path = usePathname() --- <p>You are on { { path } }</p> note"},{"u":"en/hooks/use-query/","t":"useQuery & params","s":"Hooks","k":["usequery","use pathname","search params","query string","parameters","url","router"],"b":"Reading the URL in your pages is one of the most common tasks. Nexy makes it a one-liner. Query parameters For a URL like /search?q=python&page=2, useQuery returns the parameters: --- from nexy import useQuery query = useQuery() q = query.get(\"q\") page = query.get(\"page\", default=\"1\") --- <p>Searching for { { q } }, page { { page } }</p> Search params & pathname --- from nexy import usePathname, useSearchParams path = usePathname() params = useSearchParams() --- <p>{ { path } }?{ { params } }</p> Dynamic route params On a dynamic route like [slug].nexy, the parameter is available to the page - it's matched from the URL segment. tip"},{"u":"en/i18n/overview/","t":"Internationalization","s":"i18n","k":["i18n","internationalization","translation","locale","language","overview","multilingual"],"b":"Nexy is multilingual by design. Strings live in locale files, and your pages pull them with t(). How it works A locale is a language code like `en`, `fr`, `ar`, `zh`… Translated strings are stored per locale. `t(key)` returns the string for the active locale. `trans()` and `useLocale` give you more control. Example --- from nexy import t, useLocale hello = t(\"hello\") lang = useLocale() --- <p>{ { hello } } - locale: { { lang } }</p> tip"},{"u":"en/i18n/translate/","t":"Translating content","s":"i18n","k":["translate","translation","content","mdx","locales","nexy translate","i18n"],"b":"Content files (markdown, mdx) can be translated with the nexy translate command. Naming convention A translated page keeps its name with a locale suffix: about.mdx becomes about.fr.mdx, about.ar.mdx, and so on. Run a translation nexy translate --locales fr,ar,zh Nexy parses each content file into segments (frontmatter, code blocks, text), translates the text segments, and writes the locale variant. Inline elements (links, emphasis) are protected during translation. A temporary connection failure fails over per file, not the whole run. Code blocks stay untouched. note"},{"u":"en/config/nexyconfig/","t":"nexyconfig.py","s":"Configuration","k":["config","nexyconfig","configuration","settings","setup","port","router","aliases"],"b":"Every Nexy project has a nexyconfig.py at its root. A class extending NexyConfigModel holds all your settings. A typical config from nexy.core.models import NexyConfigModel from src.apps.app_module import AppModule from nexy.frontend import react, vue class NexyConfig(NexyConfigModel): useRouter = AppModule useFF = [react(), vue()] useAliases = {\"@\": \"src/components\"} usePort = 3000 useHost = \"0.0.0.0\" useTitle = \"My app\" useDocs = True useVite = True excludeDirs = [\"templates\", \"docs\"] What you can set Key Purpose useRouter The router (AppModule for modular). useFF Frontend frameworks to enable. useAliases Import aliases like `@` → `src/components`. usePort / useHost Dev and start server defaults. useTitle Default page title. useDocs Enable the docs integration. useVite Serve client bundles through Vite. excludeDirs Folders excluded from routing. tip"},{"u":"en/frontend/frameworks/","t":"Frontend frameworks","s":"Frontend","k":["frontend","react","vue","svelte","solid","preact","framework","tsx","client"],"b":"Nexy supports the most popular frontend frameworks: React, Vue, Svelte, Solid and Preact. Enable a framework Activate them in nexyconfig.py: from nexy.frontend import react, vue, svelte, solid, preact class NexyConfig(NexyConfigModel): useFF = [react(), vue()] Framework pages as routes With FBR, .tsx, .vue and .svelte files under src/routes/ are routes too. They can be server-rendered or client-only. How rendering works During `build`, pages are server-rendered with esbuild (no Vite required). If a page can't be rendered on the server, it falls back to a client placeholder and shows a clear warning. Pages are built in parallel - each file independently. tip"},{"u":"en/frontend/vite-hmr/","t":"Vite & hot reload","s":"Frontend","k":["vite","hmr","hot reload","hot module","client","bundle","dev"],"b":"During development, client assets are served through Vite for the best hot-reload experience. What Vite does for you Instant hot module replacement (HMR) for your components. Dependency pre-bundling for faster startup. Modern JavaScript out of the box. Enable / disable class NexyConfig(NexyConfigModel): useVite = True # dev server via Vite useVite = False # fall back to esbuild Production In production there is no dev server. nexy build bundles the client with esbuild - fast, parallel, and dependency-light. note"},{"u":"en/orm/overview/","t":"Data layer","s":"ORM & data","k":["orm","database","model","sqlite","sqlmodel","sqlalchemy","tortoise","data"],"b":"Nexy works with your favorite Python ORM. SQLite, SQLModel, SQLAlchemy and Tortoise are first-class citizens. Choose an ORM ORM Best when SQLite Zero-setup local database, prototypes, small apps. SQLModel Pydantic-style models, great DX. SQLAlchemy Full control, mature ecosystem. Tortoise Async-first ORM. Models in src/models/ Put your models in src/models/ (FBR) or next to the module (modular). They're regular ORM models - nothing special required. Migrations Nexy scaffolds an Alembic-based setup so you can evolve your schema safely. See migrations."},{"u":"en/orm/migrations/","t":"Migrations","s":"ORM & data","k":["migration","alembic","schema","database","nexy migrate","models","generate"],"b":"Migrations let you evolve your database schema safely as your models change. The flow Edit your models in `src/models/`. Generate a migration describing the change. Apply it with `nexy migrate`. Run migrations nexy migrate # apply pending migrations to the database Good habits Generate one migration per logical change. Review the generated SQL before applying it. Keep migrations in version control. Never hand-edit an applied migration - create a new one. warn"},{"u":"en/security/overview/","t":"Security overview","s":"Security","k":["security","auth","authentication","sessions","cookies","guards","protection","csrf"],"b":"Secure by default. Nexy integrates authentication, sessions, cookies and guards so you don't reinvent protection. What's included Sessions via `useSession`. Cookies via `useCookies`. Authentication helpers and guards for protected routes. CSRF protection for forms. Password hashing and JWT support (documented in the full guide). Guards A guard protects a route: if the visitor isn't allowed, they're redirected or rejected. Attach guards to routes you want to protect. Keep in mind Always store secrets (API keys, signing keys) in environment variables, never in code. Use HTTPS in production. Set sensible cookie flags (HttpOnly, Secure) for session cookies. tip"},{"u":"en/security/sessions/","t":"Sessions & cookies","s":"Security","k":["session","cookies","useSession","useCookies","login","state","csrf"],"b":"Sessions keep your users logged in across requests. Cookies carry small pieces of state. Read the session --- from nexy import useSession session = useSession() user_id = session.get(\"user_id\") --- <p>{ { user_id or \"anonymous\" } }</p> Read cookies --- from nexy import useCookies cookies = useCookies() pref = cookies.get(\"theme\") --- <p>Theme: { { pref or \"default\" } }</p> Forms & CSRF Nexy's Form component wires CSRF protection for you. Always use it for state-changing forms. Best practices Store only an identifier in the session, not sensitive data. Sign session cookies in production. Regenerate sessions after login (defense against fixation)."},{"u":"en/deploy/build/","t":"Production build","s":"Build & deploy","k":["build","production","deploy","ssg","ssr","static","optimize","assets"],"b":"nexy build produces a production-ready bundle from your source. Build it nexy build What the build produces Server-rendered HTML for each page (SSG/SSR). Optimized client bundles. A clean build output directory ready to serve. Handling failures A page that fails to build becomes a warning with the real error message - other pages still build. Client-only pages get a placeholder <div>. Before deploying Run the test suite. Run `nexy build` and fix the warnings. Test `nexy start` locally with the built output. Deploy the build output to your server."},{"u":"en/deploy/github-pages/","t":"Deploy on GitHub Pages","s":"Build & deploy","k":["github pages","deploy","static","hosting","gh pages","site","free"],"b":"GitHub Pages hosts static sites for free. Since a Nexy site can be built to pure HTML, it fits perfectly. 1. Build your site nexy build 2. Publish the build output Create a `gh-pages` branch containing the build output, or Point Pages at a folder of your repo (Settings → Pages → Source). 3. SPA routing note If your site uses hash-based routing (#/en/page), no server configuration is needed - it works as-is on GitHub Pages. tip"},{"u":"en/glossary/terms/","t":"Glossary of terms","s":"Glossary","k":["glossary","terms","definition","fbr","ssr","ssg","hmr","route","template","frontmatter","scaffold"],"b":"Every term you'll meet in the Nexy docs, explained in plain language. Term Meaning FBR File-Based Routing - routes defined by the file tree under `src/routes/`. Route A URL your app responds to (e.g. `/blog/hello`). Dynamic segment A variable part of a URL, written `[slug]`. Layout A wrapper page that applies to child pages (navbar, footer). Frontmatter The Python block at the top of a `.nexy` file, between `---` delimiters. Template The markup part of a page, rendered with Jinja2. Hook A `use*` Python function that gives access to request data. SSG Static Site Generation - HTML produced at build time. SSR Server-Side Rendering - HTML produced per request. HMR Hot Module Replacement - the browser updates without a full reload. Scaffold Generating a complete starter project. tip"},{"u":"en/troubleshooting/common-issues/","t":"Common issues","s":"Troubleshooting","k":["troubleshooting","fix","issue","error","port","already initialized","template","cache","debug"],"b":"A quick guide to the most common problems and their fixes. The port is already in use Nexy tries the next available port automatically. To force one, pass --port or set usePort in nexyconfig.py. nexy init says the project is already initialized Nexy refuses to overwrite an existing project. Point init at a fresh directory, or use nexy new <name> for a new folder. Unknown template The --template name is validated first. List the bundled ones: web-fbr, web-modular, api-fbr, api-modular. Framework templates are fetched from GitHub - you need network. A page doesn't hot-reload Restart `nexy dev` after editing `nexyconfig.py`. Check the file extension is watched (`*.nexy`, `*.py`, `*.mdx`, `*.tsx`, `*.vue`, `*.svelte`). Look at the browser overlay for a compilation error. Stale caches Dev cleans __pycache__ automatically. If something looks stale, restart the dev server - caches are rebuilt. warn"},{"u":"en/reference/hooks/","t":"Hooks reference","s":"Reference","k":["reference","hooks","api","usepathname","userouter","usequery","usesession","usecookies","usesearchparams","usetoc","useviews"],"b":"Every Nexy hook, its signature and what it returns. Hooks are the way your pages speak to the request context. Request hooks tip"},{"u":"en/reference/decorators/","t":"Decorators reference","s":"Reference","k":["reference","decorator","injectable","i18n","di","dependency injection","singleton"],"b":"Class-level decorators that give your code framework powers. Dependency injection Internationalization"},{"u":"en/reference/routers/","t":"Routers reference","s":"Reference","k":["reference","router","fbr","modular","dispatcher","route"],"b":"The two routing strategies Nexy ships with, and how to dispatch between them. File-Based Router Modular Router"},{"u":"en/reference/cli/","t":"CLI reference","s":"Reference","k":["reference","cli","command","nexy new","nexy init","nexy dev","nexy start","nexy build"],"b":"Every nexy command at a glance. Scaffolding Development"},{"u":"en/changelog/releases/","t":"Release notes","s":"Changelog","k":["changelog","release","version","update","2.1.1","announcement"],"b":"Every Nexy release, from the latest to the first. Follow along with each new version. 2.1.1 Current stable release. Fixed `TemplateFormatter.format_attributes` edge cases for empty attribute values. Updated docs landing to a full app-style hero. Client-only rendering guards for Vue, Svelte and Preact SSR. 2.1.0 Added the `useViews` hook. Improved Vite HMR reliability under Windows. SSG builds now run per-file esbuild builds in parallel. 2.0.0 The two-router release. Introduced Modular Routing alongside File-Based Routing. `@Injectable` with singleton / request / transient scopes. `@i18n` class-level translations. tip"}]};
